Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
When the disciples came to Jesus, they asked him, saying, "Who is the greatest in the kingdom of the heavens?"
And Jesus called a small child, and he set him in their midst.
Jesus said, "What I tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't, you will never enter the kingdom of heaven."
When someone like this child becomes humble, he is the greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Whoever receives a child like him in my name receives me.
But if one of these little ones who believe in me is led astray, it would be better for him to have a great millstone fastened around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How intense is the trial when a man is seduced by wealth and children * And that when the time of his return to me comes, then you will be informed by me about what you used to do * Nay, but He strengthened them in their life of this world and on the day when the witnesses will stand forth * The day when it will not profit the wrongdoers their pleading, and theirs will be the curse and theirs will be an evil abode
If your hand or foot causes you to stumble into sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with only one hand or one foot, than to be thrown into the eternal fire with both hands and feet. The fire that has been prepared for the devil and his angels is never extinguished.
If your right eye causes you to stumble, tear it out and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of God with only one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into hell. The parable of the lost sheep.
Look and do not despise one of these little ones. Behold, I say to you, their angels in heaven always see the face of my Father who is in heaven.
Translated
What do you think? Let's suppose a man owns one hundred sheep, and one of them goes astray. Will he not leave the ninety-nine sheep on the mountains? Will he not go in search of the one that went astray?
I'm not going to bother updating about it, it's not real. If he found that sheep, he's happier with it than the 99 that didn't go astray.
And thus your heavenly Father will not wish that any of these little ones should perish. And if your brother wrongs you, rebuke him.
If your brother wrongs you, go to him alone and tell him his wrong against you. If he listens to you, you have gained your brother.
But what if he does not listen to you? Take with you one or two more, so that 'by the mouth of two or three witnesses every matter may be established.' Deu 19:15
But if he refuses to listen to them as well, then tell the church. And if he refuses to listen to the church as well, then let him be to you as a Gentile or a tax collector.
What I am telling you now is true, and what you close up on earth is closed up in heaven, and what you open up on earth is opened up in heaven.
And as I have told you before, if two of you agree on earth about anything that they may ask, it will be done for them by my Father who is in heaven. I will do it for you.
Where two or three are gathered in my name, there am I among them. The servant who did not have mercy.
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how many times must I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The Kingdom of the Heavens resembles a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants regarding the money they owed him.
And the king began to do that, when a man was brought to him who owed him millions of dollars.
The man was unable to pay, so his master ordered him to be sold, along with his wife, his children, and everything he possessed, in order to recover the debt he owed.
The slave fell on his knees before him. "Give me time," he pleaded. "I will pay you back everything."
His master was pleased with his good luck, so he freed him and forgave him what he owed him.
But that servant went out and found one of the servants who owed him some dollars. So he seized him and choked him, saying, "Pay back what you owe me!"
The other server fell to his knees. "Give me a break," he pleaded. "I'll pay you back."
But the first servant refused. Instead, he went and had the man thrown into prison. There he would stay until he paid back what he owed.
The other servants saw what happened. It greatly disturbed them. So they went and told their master everything that had happened.
Then the master called his first servant to him and said to him, O my evil servant, I have forgiven you all your debt because you asked me to.
Did you not love to be the one who had forgiven the slave to the other, as I have had mercy on you?
His master was angry with him and handed him over to the jailers. He will be punished until he pays back everything he owes.
Thus does my heavenly Father do to each of you, if you do not forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea beyond the Jordan.
Large crowds followed him. He healed the sick there.
Some Pharisees came to test him. They asked, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for just any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and shall cleave to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
Lisa and Jessica are no longer two, but one body. What God has joined together, let no one separate.
They said: "Why then did Moses command that a man should repudiate his wife with a writing and send her away from him?"
Jesus answered, "Moses permitted you to divorce your wives because of the hardness of your hearts. But it was not like this from the beginning."
This is what I am telling you. Whoever divorces his wife and marries another is an adulterer. A man may divorce his wife if she is not faithful to him.
The disciples said to him, "If that is the case between husband and wife, it is better not to marry."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone accepts this saying, but only those for whom God has granted the gift of celibacy."
Some men cannot have children because they were born that way. And some, people made them that way. And others, they made themselves that way to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept living like this, let them do so." Little children are brought forward to Jesus.
Some people brought little children to Jesus. They wanted him to lay his hands on them and pray for them. But the disciples told the people to go away.
Jesus said: "Let the little children come to me, and do not hinder them, for the kingdom of heaven belongs to such as these."
Jesus laid his hands on them, and he departed from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man.
A man came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to have eternal life?"
Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "There is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom of heaven, then keep the commandments."
Which? asked the man. Jesus said, *«You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness»*.
Honor your father and your mother (Exodus 20:12-16; Deuteronomy 5:16-20) and "love your neighbor as yourself" (Leviticus 19:18).
My precise translation: "I have obeyed all those commandments," said the young man. "What more must I do?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor, and you will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard that, he left sadly. He was very rich.
Then Jesus said to his disciples: "This is a truth I tell you, it is difficult for the rich to enter the kingdom of God."
And I tell you again, it is easier for a camel to go through the eye of a needle, than for a rich man to enter the kingdom of God.
When the disciples heard this, they were utterly amazed and said, "Then who can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "This is impossible for people, but with God all things are possible."
Peter answered him, "We have left everything and followed you! What then will be ours?"
Jesus said to them, "What I am about to tell you is true; when all things are renewed, the Son of Man will sit on his glorious throne. And all of you who have followed me will also sit on twelve thrones, judging the twelve tribes of Israel."
Whoever leaves behind a house or family or wealth, it is because of me that he inherits it, and he is rewarded a hundredfold by the Divine Presence, eternally.
But many of the first will be last; and many of the last will be first.
The story of the workers in the vineyard.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man, a landowner, who went out early in the morning to hire laborers for his vineyard.
He agreed to pay them the usual wage for a day's work. Then he sent them into his vineyard.
At about nine o'clock in the morning he went out again. He saw others standing in the marketplace doing nothing.
He said to them, "You also, go and work in my vineyard. I will pay you the right wage."
So they went. And he also went out around an hour of the morning and after the afternoon, and he did so as well.
About five o'clock he went out and found others standing. He asked them, "Why do you stand here all day long and do no work?"
They said to him, "Because no one has hired us." And he said to them, "You also go into my vineyard."
And when evening came, the owner of the vineyard said to the manager of the workers, "Call the workers so that each one may be given his wage, beginning with the last, those who were hired at the end of the day, and then those who were hired first."
The workers who were hired around five o'clock arrived. Each of them received their usual day's wage.
When the first group's workers came, he hired them for a fair wage. He gave each of them their day's wage, and they went away happy.
Upon receiving it, they began complaining about the landlord.
They said, "These people who were recently hired have only worked for one hour, yet you have paid them the same as you pay us. We are the ones who have done most of the work, and we have spent the whole day under the scorching sun."
The employer answered them and said to one of them: "My friend, I am dealing with you fairly, aren't I? Did you not agree to work for your usual daily wage?
Take your money and leave. I want to give those whom I have appointed to do the final work the same wage that I gave you.
Aren't I the owner of my money and I do with it whatever I want? Do you feel that you are betrayed because I gave generously to others?
Those who were last will become first, and those who were first will become last. Jesus was teaching his disciples for the third time about his upcoming crucifixion.
Jesus was going up to Jerusalem. And on the way, He took the twelve aside to speak to them.
He said, "We went up to Jerusalem, and the Son of Man will be delivered over to the chief priests and the scribes. And they will condemn him to death."
Then they hand him over to the pagans. They mock him, scourge him, and crucify him. And on the third day, he rises from the dead!
The mother of my sons Zebedee came to Jesus, and her two sons followed her. She fell down at his knees and asked him for a request.
"What do you want?" Jesus asked her. And she answered, "Promise me that one of my sons will sit in your kingdom on your right, and the other on your left."
Jesus said to them, "You do not know what you are asking. Can you drink the cup that I am to drink?" They said to him, "Yes."
Jesus said to them, "Assuredly, you will drink from the cup that I drink from; and you will be seated on My right and on My left, and the 'least' will become the 'greatest,' and everyone will be a 'king' and will rule over his own 'kingdom,' and you will 'rule' over the 'twelve' 'tribes' of 'Israel,' and everyone who has 'abandoned' his 'house' or his 'wife' or his 'children' or his 'lands' because of Me and for the sake of the 'Gospel,' will receive a 'hundred' 'times' as much in 'this' 'life' (houses and brothers and sisters and mothers and children and lands), and in the age to come, eternal life."
The other ten pupils had heard about this; they were angry with the two brothers.
Jesus gathered them and said to them: "You know that the rulers of the Gentiles lord it over them, and their great men exercise authority over them. But it shall not be so among you; but whoever desires to become great among you, let him be your servant, and whoever desires to be first among you, let him be your slave, even as the Son of Man was not to be served but to serve, and to give His life as a ransom for many."
Do not be like that; but whoever wants to become great among you must be your servant, and whoever wants to be first must be your slave, just as the Son of Man did not come to be served, but to serve, and to give his life as a ransom for many.
And whoever wants to be the first, let him be your servant.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give His life as a ransom for many, who are being freed from it.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A great crowd followed him.
