The crowd ordered them to be silent. But the two men cried out all the more, saying, "O Lord, O son of David, have mercy on us!"
Jesus stood and called them, saying, "What do you want Me to do for you?"
Our master, we hope to be enlightened.
Jesus found in their hearts great compassion, so he touched their eyes, and immediately they saw, so they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
And after they drew near to Jerusalem, and came to Bethphage, at the Mount of Olives, Jesus sent two disciples, saying to them:
He said to them, "Go to the village before you. And as soon as you enter it, you will find a donkey's foal tied there. Its mother will be with it. Untie them and bring them to me."
If anyone tells you anything, say the master needs them. The owner will send them immediately.
This took place to fulfill what was spoken by the prophet.
Say to the daughter of Zion, Behold, your king comes to you, gentle, and riding on an donkey, on a colt, the daughter of a donkey. (Zechariah 9:9)
The disciples went and did what Jesus had commanded them.
They brought a donkey and a young donkey, and they put their clothes on them, and Jesus sat on the clothes.
A large crowd of people spread their coats on the road. Others cut branches from trees and spread them on the road.
Some people spread their cloaks on the road, and others spread leafy branches from the trees. The crowds who went ahead of him and those who followed kept shouting: "Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "Hosanna in the highest!"
And when Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city was stirred up, saying, "Who is this?"
The crowd answered, "This is Jesus, the prophet from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus drove out the money changers from the temple
Jesus entered the temple and began to drive out all those who were selling and buying in it. He overturned the tables of the moneychangers and the chairs of those who sold doves.
He said to them: "It is written: 'My house will be called a house of prayer,'" (Isaiah 56:7) and "'You have made it a den for thieves,'" (Jeremiah 7:11)
And the blind and the lame and the diseased came to him in the temple, and he healed them.
And the chief priests and the scribes of the law saw the wonderful things that he did, and the children also who were in the temple, crying out, "O save, O save, O save, O our Savior, O our King, O David, O David, O David, O our David." And they were very angry.
"Do you hear what these children are saying?" they asked him. Jesus said, "Yes. Have you never read: 'From the lips of children and infants you have ordained praise'?" (Psalm 8:2)
Then Jesus left the crowd and went away from there to the house. A large crowd followed him, and he healed many who were sick with various diseases. And he also cast out demons by a word, and he healed
Early in the morning, Jesus was going back to Jerusalem. He was hungry.
He saw a fig tree on the road. He turned to it and did not find anything in it except leaves. And he said to it: "Let nothing of the fruit ever return in you!" So the tree withered like a spear in the eye.
The disciples were astonished at this and said, "How did the fig tree wither so quickly!"
Jesus answered, "Truly, I tell you, if you have faith and do not doubt, you will not only do what has been done to the fig tree, but even if you tell this mountain, 'Go, throw yourself into the sea,' it will be done. And whatever you ask in prayer, you will receive, if you have faith."
If you believe, you will receive what you ask for in prayer. Indeed, the authority of Jesus is absolute.
Jesus entered the temple courtyard. As he taught there, the chief priests and the elders of the people came to him and said, "By what authority are you doing these things? And who gave you this authority?"
Jesus answered, "I will also ask you one question, and answer me. Then I will tell you by what authority I do these things."
Where did the source of John's baptism come from? From heaven or from men? They discussed this among themselves and said, "If we say, 'From heaven,' he will say to us, 'Why then did you not believe him?'"
But what if we say: "Of the men"? We are afraid of people. Everyone believes that John was a prophet.
They answered him, "We do not know." Jesus said to them, "I also will not tell you by what authority I do these things."
What do you think about this? A certain man had two sons. He went to the first and said, "Son, go to work today in the vineyard."
"I'm not going to go," the son answered. But he changed his mind afterwards and went.
Then the father went to the other son and said the same thing to him, and the son replied: "I will, Father." But he did not.
Which of the two sons did the will of his father?" They said, "The first." Jesus said to them, "Truly I say to you, that tax collectors and prostitutes enter into the kingdom of God before you.
The story of the tenants.
Listen to another story. A man owned a piece of land, and he planted a vineyard in it. And he surrounded it with a fence. And he dug a press basin in it. And he built a watchtower in it. Then he rented the farm to some farmers and left on a journey.
And when the time of harvest came, he sent his servants to the tenants. He told the servants to gather his share of the fruit.
But the tenants seized his slaves. They beat one of them. And they killed another. And they threw the third to the ground with stones until he died.
Then the man sent other slaves to the farmers. And this time he sent a greater number than the first time. So the farmers treated them likewise.
Finally, he sent them his son. He said, "They will respect my son."
But the tenants saw the son coming. And they said to each other, "This is the one who will inherit all the master of the house's possessions someday. Let's kill him. And everything will be ours."
They took him, brought him out of the vineyard, and killed him.
When the owner of the vineyard returns, what will he do to those tenants?
Those evil people will be destroyed, they answered. "Then the vineyard will be let out to other tenants, who will render to him the fruits in their seasons."
Jesus said to them, "Have you not read what was written: 'The stone which the builders rejected has become the head of the corner. This was in the Lord's doing, and is wonderful in our eyes'?" (Psalm 118: 22, 23)
Therefore I tell you, the kingdom of God will be taken away from you and given to a nation that will produce its fruit.
Everyone who falls on that stone will be broken into pieces. But the stone will crush whoever falls on it.
The chief priests and the Pharisees heard reports about Jesus. They realized he was speaking about them.
So they looked for a way to arrest him, but they feared the crowd, because the people believed that Jesus was a prophet.
The Wedding Dinner Story
And Jesus also said to them, "Another parable I shall speak to you:
This is what the kingdom of heaven is like. A king prepared a wedding feast for his son.
He sent his servants to those invited to the dinner. The servants told them to come. But they refused.
Then he also sent some of his servants, saying, "Tell those who are invited, 'Behold, my dinner is prepared, my oxen and fatlings are killed, and everything is ready.' Come to the feast."
But the people paid no attention. This one went to his field and that one went to his business.
The rest of the people seized his servants. They treated them badly and then killed them.
The king was furiously angry. He sent his army to eliminate them. So they killed those murderers and burned their city.
Then the king said to the servants, "The wedding dinner is ready for the newlyweds. But the invited guests whom I invited were not worthy to attend."
Go out into the street corners. Invite everyone you find to dinner.
So the servants went out into the streets and gathered everyone they found, whether good or evil. And soon the wedding hall was filled with guests.
The king entered to see the guests. He noticed a man who was not wearing the wedding clothes.
"My friend," he asked, "how did you get in here without wedding clothes?" The man could not say anything.
Then the king said to his servants, "Tie his hands and feet. Throw him outside into the darkness. There is where there will be weeping and the grinding of teeth."
Many are called, but few are chosen. Is it right for us to pay taxes to Caesar?
The Pharisees went out and began to plot how they might seize Jesus by what he said.
They sent their disciples to him. They sent the Herodians with them. And they said, "Teacher, we know that you are a truthful man, and you teach the way of God in truth, and you do not care for anyone, for you do not regard the faces of people."
Tell us then, what do you think? Is it right that we pay taxes to Caesar or not?
But Jesus perceived their wickedness, and said, "Why do you tempt me, you hypocrites?"
Show me the coin that people use to pay taxes. So they brought him a silver coin.
He asked them, "Whose picture is this? And whose words are these?"
They said, "To Caesar." And He said to them, "Give to Caesar the things that are Caesar's, and to God the things that are God's."
When they heard this, they were astonished and went on their way. Marriage, when the dead rise.
On the same day, the scribes came to Jesus with a question. For they did not believe in the resurrection of the dead.
They said, "Teacher, this is what Moses said: 'If a man dies without having children, his brother must marry his widow. He must have a child by her who will carry his brother's name.'"
There were seven brothers among us. The first one got married and died. Since he had no children, he bequeathed his wife to his brother.
The same thing happened with the second brother, the third brother, and so on, until the seventh brother.
Finally, the woman died.
And now, when the dead rise, to whom will she be wife? For all seven married her.
Jesus answered, "You are going astray, because you do not know the Scriptures nor the power of God."
If the dead were to rise from their graves, they would not get married. Nor would their fathers give them away in marriage either. They would be like the angels in heaven.
What about the dead who rise from their graves? Have you not read what God has said to you?
He said, "I am the God of Abraham. I am the God of Isaac. And I am the God of Jacob" (Exodus 3:6). He is not the God of the dead, but of the living
The attendees were amazed at what he was teaching them. Which of the following commandments is the most important?
When the Pharisees heard that the Sadducees could not answer Jesus, the Pharisees gathered together.
And there was among them an expert in the law, and he asked Jesus a test question:
The teacher, asked him, "What is the most important commandment in the Law?"
Jesus answered, "Love the Lord your God with all your heart, with all your soul, and with all your mind." (Deuteronomy 6:5)
This is the first and most important commandment.
And the second is like it: 'And you shall love your neighbor as yourself.' (Leviticus 19:18)
All that is written in the law and the prophets depends on these two commandments." Who is the Son of Christ?
The Pharisees met together, and Jesus asked them,
What do you think about the Messiah? Whose son is he? They answered him: The son of David.
He said to them, "Then why does David call him Lord? Because the Holy Spirit spoke through David himself. David said,
My master said to my lord, "Sit at my right hand, until I make your enemies your footstool." (Psalm 110:1)
If David called him Lord, how can he be David's son?
No one could answer him with a single word. And from that day on, no one dared to ask him a question.
Jesus rules over the Pharisees and the scribes.
Jesus speaks to the crowds and to the disciples.
He said, "The scribes and the Pharisees sit on the seat of Moses."
You must obey them. Do everything they ask of you. But do not do what they do. They do not apply what they say.
They bind heavy loads and place them on the shoulders of people. But they do not move a finger to raise them with the smallest of their fingers.
All they do is for the sake of being seen by others. On their foreheads and arms they place small boxes containing verses from the Bible. They make the boxes very wide. And they make the tapes of the coats very long.
They like to sit in the seat of honor at banquets. And they like to be in the chest at assemblies.
They like to be greeted with "Peace be upon you" in the markets of the country. And they are pleased when people call them "my Lord".
But it is not right to call you 'Rabbi'. You have one teacher, and you are all brothers.
Do not call anyone on earth your father. For you have one Father, and He is in heaven.
There is no one to call him "our Lord." You have one Lord, and that is Christ.
The most important person among you will be your servant.
He who exalts himself will be humbled, and he who is humbled will be exalted.
Woe to you, you scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you shut the kingdom of heaven in people's faces. You yourselves do not enter, nor will you let those enter who are trying.
Translated
Woe to you, you scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you are like a sounding brass or a tinkling cymbal. For when you speak in the synagogue, you are like a wise man speaking to fools. But when you are alone, you are like a foolish man speaking to himself. And when you are in the streets, you are like a dead man carried out to be buried. For you are like a whitewashed tomb, which on the outside appears beautiful, but inside is full of dead men's bones and all kinds of uncleanliness. Even so, on the outside you appear righteous to men, but inside you are full of hypocrisy and lawlessness. Woe to you, you scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you build the tombs of the prophets and decorate the monuments of the righteous. And you say, 'If we had lived in the days of our fathers, we would not have participated with them in shedding the blood of the prophets.' So you testify against yourselves that you are sons of those who murdered the prophets. Fill up, then, the measure of your fathers' sins! You serpents, you offspring of vipers, how can you escape the sentence of hell? Therefore, behold, I am sending you prophets and wise men and scribes, some of whom you will kill and crucify, and some of whom you will scourge in your synagogues and persecute from town to town. And so upon you will come all the righteous blood shed upon the earth, from the blood of righteous Abel to the blood of Zechariah, the son of Barachiah, whom you murdered between the temple and the altar. Truly I tell you, all these things will come upon this generation. O Jerusalem, Jerusalem, the city that kills the prophets and stones those who are sent to her! How often would I have gathered your children together as a hen gathers her brood under her wings, but you would not! See, your house is left to you, desolate! For I tell you, you will not see me again until you say, 'Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord.' And Jesus left and went away.
How blind you are in your misleading! You who guide the blind! For you say: If anyone takes an oath by the temple of God, that is nothing; but whoever swears by the gold of the temple, he is bound by his oath!
You are blind and foolish! What is more important? The gold or the temple that sanctifies the gold?
You also said: "If someone swears by the altar, it is not binding upon him. But whoever swears by the offering to be laid upon it, he may be bound by the responsibility."
O sightless ones! What is more important? Is it the gift or the altar that sanctifies the gift?
For he who swears by the Jerusalem swears by Jerusalem and by everything in it.
And everyone who swears by the temple swears by the temple and by the God who dwells in it.
And whoever swears by the heavens has indeed sworn by the throne of Allah and whoever is upon it.
Bewail yourselves, O teachers of the true faith and the Phrygians, ye hypocrites! Ye pay tithe on the mint and the cummin and the dill; but ye have left undone the weightier matters of the law, judgment and mercy and faith. Ye should have done these things, and not have left those undone.
You are the leaders of misguidance! You strain out your food to remove even the smallest bit of grass, but you swallow a camel whole!
Woe to you, you scribes and Pharisees, you hypocrites! For you cleanse the outside of the cup and the dish, but inside they are full of greed and wickedness. You blind Pharisee! First cleanse the inside of the cup and the dish, so that the outside of them may also be clean.
You blind Pharisee! First cleanse the inside of the cup and of the platter, that the outside of them may also be clean.
