Jesus (peace be upon him) will rise from the dead.
It was now dawn on the first day of the week. Mary Magdalene and the other Mary went to look at the tomb.
There was a strong earthquake. And an angel descended from heaven. The angel went to the tomb. And he rolled away the stone and sat on it.
His body was shining like lightning. His clothes were as white as snow.
The guards were terribly afraid of him, trembling and becoming like the dead.
The angel said to the two women: "Do not be afraid. I know that you are looking for Jesus of Nazareth, who was crucified."
What is here! He has arisen as he said! Come and see the place where he was lying.
Go quickly and tell his disciples: 'He has been raised from the dead! He is going ahead of you into Galilee. There you will see him.' 'Look, I have told you!'
The women hurried to the tomb, fearful and bewildered. They ran to give the disciples the good news.
Suddenly Jesus appeared to them, saying, "Peace be to you!" And they approached and held His feet and worshiped Him.
Then Jesus said to them, "Do not be afraid. Go and tell my brothers to go to Galilee. There they will see me." The guards went out and, informing the chief priests of all that had happened, they went with the elders and took counsel to put Jesus to death.
And while they were on the road, some of the guards entered the city and reported to the chief priests everything that had happened.
When the High Priests met with the elders, they made a plan. They gave the soldiers a large sum of money.
They told the soldiers: "We want you to say: 'His disciples came at night and stole his body while we were sleeping.'"
If the governor hears this report, we will financially satisfy him. This will keep you safe from trouble.
The soldiers obeyed their orders and took the money. To this day, the Jews continue to tell this story. Jesus's last commands to his disciples.
Then the disciples set out on the Sunday, the eleventh, for Galilee, to the mountain to which Jesus had instructed them.
And when he saw them prostrating to him, some of them remained doubting.
Then Jesus, when he came, spoke, saying, 'All authority has been given to me in heaven and on earth.'
Go therefore and make disciples of all nations. Baptize them in the name of the Father and of the Son and of the Holy Spirit.
Teach them to obey everything I have commanded them, and know that I am always with you to the end of the world.
He is preparing the way, making ready the Christ. He is called John the Baptist.
This is the beginning of the gospel of Jesus Christ, the Son of God, as a good news of his birth.
A long time ago, the prophet Isaiah wrote: "Behold, I will send my messenger, and he will prepare the way before you" (Malachi 3:1)
He calls in the wilderness, a prepared road for the Lord; make in the desert a highway for our God. (Isaiah 40:3)
And so John came. He was baptizing people in the wilderness. He was preaching repentance, baptizing them as they confessed their sins, and then God forgave them their sins.
All the people of Judea and Jerusalem went out to him; and they confessed their sins, and he baptized them in the river Jordan.
And John wore clothes made of camel's hair and a leather belt around his waist. He ate wild locusts from the desert, honey, and wild food.
This is what John was proclaiming: "I am not worthy to untie the thong of his sandals."
I baptize you with water, but he who comes after me is mightier than I, whose sandals I am not worthy to untie. He will baptize you with the Holy Spirit.
And at that time Jesus came from Nazareth of Galilee. And John the Baptist baptized him in the Jordan.
And while he was coming up out of the water, he saw the heavens being torn open. And the Spirit descended on him like a dove.
And a voice from heaven spoke to him, saying, "You are my beloved Son; with you I am well pleased."
And the Holy Spirit immediately drove Jesus out into the wilderness.
He was in the wilderness for forty days. And the devil tempted him there. And wild beasts did not harm him. Angels took care of him. Jesus chose the first disciples.
And after John was imprisoned, Jesus went to Galilee preaching the gospel of God.
He said: "The time has come, the Kingdom of God is near. Therefore, repent of your sins and believe in the good news!"
One day Jesus was walking by the Sea of Galilee. There he saw Simon and his brother Andrew casting a net into the sea. They were fishermen.
Come and follow me, said Jesus. "I will make you fishers of men."
So they left the fish and followed him until the time.
Then Jesus walked a little further, and saw James the son of Zebedee and his brother John. They were in the boat repairing the nets.
And after that, He called them to Him immediately. So they left their father Zebedee in the boat with the hired men, and they followed Him. The expulsion of Jesus by the unclean spirit.
And Jesus moved, with those who were with Him, to Capernaum. And He entered the synagogue on the Sabbath day, and He began to teach.
People were fascinated by his teaching. He taught them with absolute authority, not like the scholars of Islamic law.
And at that moment, a man in the congregation cried out, for an unclean spirit was clinging to him.
What do you want from us, Jesus of Nazareth? Have you come to destroy us? I know who you are. You are the Holy One of God!
"Be quiet!" Jesus said firmly. "Come out of him!"
The evil spirit violently shook the man. Then it came out of him, screaming.
And everyone was amazed at this, and they were saying to one another, "What is this speech? For with authority He commands even the unclean spirits, and they obey Him."
News of Jesus spread quickly throughout Galilee. Jesus healed many people.
Jesus withdrew from the synagogue, followed by a large crowd. They came to the house of Simon and James, Jesus' brother. James and John were with him.
Simon's mother-in-law was lying in bed, suffering from a fever. They told Jesus about her.
So he went to her, took her by the hand, and helped her rise. The fever left her, and she hastened to serve them.
That evening, after sunset, all the sick people and all those possessed by demons were brought to Jesus by the people.
All the people in the city gathered at the gate.
Jesus healed many of them, who were sick with various diseases. He also cast out many demons, but He did not allow the demons to speak because they knew who He was. Jesus' prayer in a quiet place.
It was very early in the morning, and the darkness was still hanging over the sky. Jesus got up, left the house, and went to a place where there were no people. And there he prayed.
Simon and his friends went to look for Jesus.
When they found him, they shouted, saying, "Everyone is looking for you!"
Jesus answered, "Let us go to another place, so that I may preach in the towns nearby, because I came for this purpose."
He went throughout all of Galilee, teaching in their synagogues and casting out demons. He healed many who were sick with various diseases; among them was a man who had leprosy.
A man who had a skin disease came to Jesus. He knelt before Jesus and begged him, saying, "If you are willing, you can make me clean."
Jesus was deeply moved with compassion. He reached out his hand and touched the man, saying to him, "I want to do this. Be clean!"
He was immediately cured of his illness.
Jesus immediately banished him and sternly warned the man.
He said, "Don't tell anyone about this." Then he said, "Go and present yourself to the priest and offer the sacrifices that Moses commanded. This will serve as proof to the priest and to the people that you are pure."
But the man went out and began to talk at once, and spread the news to everyone. So Jesus was no longer able to enter a city publicly. He remained outside in deserted places. But people kept coming to him from everywhere.
Jesus heals a man who could not walk.
After a few days, Jesus entered once more into Capernaum. The people had heard that he was at home,
So many people gathered that there was no more room, not even outside the door. So Jesus spoke to them the Word of God.
Four people brought a man who cannot walk, carrying him.
But they could not advance to him because of the crowds that were surrounding him. So they broke through the roof above Jesus' head, and after they had broken through, they let down the bed on which the sick man was lying.
Jesus saw their faith. He said to the man, "Son, your sins are forgiven."
Some of the scribes were sitting there. thinking,
