After that, Jesus was reclining at the table in the house of Levi. And there were tax collectors and sinners eating with him and his disciples. This was a group from the large crowd that was following Jesus.
There were some scribes of the Pharisees. When they saw Jesus eating with tax collectors and sinners, they said to his disciples, "Why does he eat with tax collectors and sinners?"
Jesus heard this, and said to them: "It is not those who are well who need a doctor, but those who are sick. I did not come to call the righteous, but sinners to repentance."
The disciples of John and the Pharisees were fasting. Some people came to Jesus and said to him, "The disciples of John fast, and the disciples of the Pharisees also. But your disciples are eating."
Jesus answered, "How can the bridegroom's attendants fast while the bridegroom is with them? But the days will come when the bridegroom will be taken away from them; then they will fast in those days. No one sews a patch of unshrunk cloth on an old garment; otherwise, the new patch will pull away from the old garment, and the tear will be worse. No one pours new wine into old wineskins. Otherwise, the new wine will burst the skins, and both the wine and the skins will be ruined. No one puts new wine into old wineskins."
But the time will come when the bridegroom will be taken from them. On that day they will go out while fasting.
People do not patch a new garment with a patch from worn-out clothes. If they do, the new will be torn by the old, and the hole will widen.
People do not pour new wine into old wineskins. For the new wine would burst the wineskins, spilling the wine and ruining the wineskins. Rather, they pour new wine into new wineskins." Jesus, Lord of the Sabbath Day.
On the Sabbath, Jesus was walking in the field with his disciples. And while they were picking out the grain, the disciples were picking the heads of grain and eating them.
The Pharisees said to Jesus, "Look! It is not lawful to do this on the Sabbath. Why are your disciples doing this?"
He answered them: Have you never read what David did? He and his men were hungry. They needed food.
This was in the time of the high priest Abiathar. David entered the house of God and ate the holy bread, which was only permitted to be eaten by the priests. David also gave some of it to his men.
Then Jesus said to them, "The Sabbath was made for man, not man for the Sabbath.
So the son of man is Lord even of the sabbath.
Healing on Saturday.
Jesus entered the synagogue again, and a man was there whose hand was withered and deformed.
Some Pharisees were looking for a reason to condemn Jesus. They were carefully observing him. How could he heal the sick on the Sabbath day?
Jesus said to the man who had a withered right hand, "Stand up in the midst of everyone."
Then Jesus asked them, "What does the Law say? What do you do on the Sabbath? Is it lawful to do good or to do evil? To save life or to kill?" But they kept silent and put no more questions to him.
Jesus directed an angry look at them and pondered deeply about the hardness of their hearts, then said to the man: "Extend your hand." So he extended it, and the hand was healed as if it were one whole, sound hand.
Then the Pharisees went out and began to plot with the Herodians against Jesus, to kill him. And the multitude followed them, and they took Jesus and killed him. And the multitude followed them, and they took Jesus and killed him. And the multitude followed them, and they took Jesus and killed him.
Jesus set out with his disciples to the Sea of Galilee, and a large crowd from Galilee followed him.
The people heard about all that Jesus was doing, and many people came to him. They came from Judea and Jerusalem and from Idumea and from the region east of the Jordan, and also from around Tyre and Sidon.
Because of the crowd, Jesus asked his disciples to prepare a small boat for him. This was to keep the crowd from him.
Jesus healed many people, so the sick were pushing to touch him.
